Certainly! Here is a JavaScript program that utilizes several advanced features, including Promises, async/await, and the Fetch API to demonstrate asynchronous operations. The code also uses ES6+ features like arrow functions, template literals, and destructuring:

```javascript
const fetchJson = async (url) => {
  try {
    const response = await fetch(url);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`Error: ${response.statusText}`);
    const data = await response.json();
    return data;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Fetching error:', error);
    throw error;
  }
};

const processData = ({ results }) => {
  return results.map(({ name, height, mass }) => ({
    name: name,
    height: parseInt(height, 10),
    mass: parseInt(mass, 10)
  }));
};

const calculateTotalMass = (characters) => {
  return characters.reduce((total, { mass }) => total + mass, 0);
};

(async () => {
  const apiUrl = 'https://swapi.dev/api/people/';
  
  try {
    const data = await fetchJson(apiUrl);
    const characters = processData(data);
    const totalMass = calculateTotalMass(characters);
    
    console.log(`Total mass of all characters: ${totalMass}`);
  } catch (error) {
    console.error('Error in main function:', error);
  }
})();
```

This program fetches data from the Star Wars API, processes the character data, and calculates the total mass of the characters, showcasing a combination of async operations, data manipulation, and modern JavaScript syntax.